course: dictionary
lesson: 651
title: Finding the right meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around a word, its context, tell you which meaning the writer wants. «Ella earned a badge for swimming.» Here a badge is a small piece of metal, plastic or cloth that shows what you have done.
explain: «Look for a safe adult, like a police officer or a shop worker with a name badge.» Here a badge shows who someone is. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence best, then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Badge can also mean a sign of something, as in «a badge of honour». Honour means respect and pride, so this is a sign that someone deserves respect. The clue is in the words next to it.
fact: A badge is a small piece of metal, plastic or cloth with a picture or words on it.
fact: People wear badges to show who they are, what they belong to or what they have done.
fact: In games and apps, a badge is a reward.
fact: A «badge of honour» is a sign of something that you should be proud of.
fact: A name badge shows your name, and police officers can show a badge to show they are official.
exercise: «Ella earned a badge for swimming.» What is a badge here? | A kind of bread | *A small piece of metal, plastic or cloth that shows what you have done | A map of the swimming pool | A bird that can swim very fast
exercise: «Look for a safe adult, like a police officer or a shop worker with a name badge.» What is a name badge? | A bag for your books | A type of school bell | A sticker on a shop window | *A small sign that shows who you are
exercise: In games and apps, what is a badge? | A kind of hat | A ticket to the next level of the school | *A reward | A broken toy
test: Someone says «a badge of honour». What does badge mean here? | A gold coin | A hard, cold piece of metal | *A sign of something, such as respect or pride | A heavy bag
test: Why do police officers and other officials show a badge? | To hide their name | To buy things in a shop | *To show who they are | To make the street look nice
exam: In «If you see a stranger in the school grounds without a badge, you tell a teacher straight away», what is a badge most likely to show? | The time of day | The colour of the school gate | *Who a person is or that they belong there | What the stranger had for lunch
